    "lawSection": "Appropriations",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S9004\n\nSPONSOR: BOYLE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act establishing a temporary state commission to\nstudy and make recommendations on the effectiveness of the AMBER Alert;\nmaking an appropriation therefor; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation will create a temporary commission to study the effec-\ntiveness of the AMBER Alert system. The commission will have three\nmonths to evaluate the current activation criteria of the AMBER Alert,\nits notification procedures once activated, and the partners and methods\nused to disseminate information. Once the study is completed, the\ncommission must provide a report and a recommendation for the legisla-\nture and governor to completely accept or reject.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Section 1 of the bill officially establishes the commission,\ncontains the subject to be examined, and requires that the commission\nsubmit a recommendation to the legislature\n\nSection 2: Determines the appointment of the thirteen members of the\ncommission and provides that no member shall receive any additional\ncompensation except their actual and necessary expenses. This section\nrequires that all agencies of the state shall assist the commission with\nsuch facilities, assistance, and data as will allow the commission to\ncarry out its functions.\n\nSection 3: This section clarifies the specific topic areas that the\ncommission must evaluate in its report. These areas include the current\nactivation criteria of the AMBER Alert, its notification procedures once\nactivated, and the partners and methods used to disseminate information.\n\nSection 4: Allows the commission to hold public or private hearings\nwithin or without the state and have all the powers of a legislative\ncommittee. The commission is entitled to receive and use facilities,\nresources, and data from any court in the state, department, or poli-\ntical subdivision thereof.\n\nSection 5: This section requires the commission to make recommendations\nand submit a report of its findings no later than three months after\nthis act shall have become law. The legislature must fully accept or\nreject its recommendations\n\nSection 6: A sum of $50,000 to pay the expenses incurred by the members\nof the commission.\n\nSection 7: Effective date; calls for the expiration of this act on the\nfirst day after the submission of the report.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, AMBER Alert Formal Activation Criteria allows police depart-\nments to choose not to activate an alert if the information provided is\ntoo vague or too much time has passed since a child's abduction. The\nAMBER Alert system is often trusted to serve as a vital resource in\nsaving the life of a child that has gone missing, yet this informal\napproach to evaluating activation criteria could lead to the child going\nmissing permanently or worse, their death.\n\nThe unfortunate and untimely death of two-year-old Jovani Ligurgo has\nhighlighted the vague requirements for issuing the AMBER Alert that\ncould have potentially saved his life. Jovani's father, John Ligurgo\nIII, abducted him after setting his home ablaze. His mother reported to\nthe police that his father may have been in possession of a gun.  The\nNew York State Police are the sole authority authorized to issue an\nAmber Alert, and despite having all of this information, decided that\nnot all criteria was met and an alert was not issued.\n\nFamilial abductions account for the highest number of child abductions\nper year, doubling the amount of stranger abductions, at over 200,000\nper year. It is critical that the effectiveness of the AMBER Alert\nsystem, and its activation criteria, be evaluated to avoid another trag-\nedy like this.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\n$50,000\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
